; Lead: BrokkAi/bifrost-packs#364 (CVE-2025-58767, GHSA-c2f4-jgmc-q2r5).
; OSV: rubygems rexml introduced 3.3.3, fixed 3.4.2.
; Product source contract: REXML::Document.new(source) parses source eagerly.
; From 3.3.3 to 3.4.1 a malformed document with repeated XML declarations makes
; the parser do unbounded work instead of rejecting it, so a sender that controls
; the document controls parse time (denial of service). 3.4.2 rejects it.
; Engine contract: the source is the return value of the reviewed
; member.web-network.ruby.net-http.get (bifrost.web-network-ruby). The sink is
; formal 0 of REXML::Document.new as declared by bifrost.ruby.rexml. That pack
; declares the same API in three shards, one per Gemfile.lock version split
; (CVE-2024-35176, CVE-2025-58767, unaffected), and each shard's member id names
; its split. The sink selects only the cve-2025-58767 member, so a project whose
; Gemfile.lock pins another rexml version binds the call to a different record
; and the run completes clean. Without a Gemfile.lock entry for rexml no shard
; activates, the call stays unbound and the run is not clean (require-model).
; rexml has been a bundled gem since Ruby 3.0, so a Bundler project that loads it
; locks it. Ruby runtime and default-gem versions are not activation evidence on
; Bifrost 0.15.2, so a project that loads rexml from the interpreter without
; Bundler stays incomplete. Only call sources are expressible: request params[]
; and env[] reads are not call shapes on 0.15.2 (bifrost-dev#11369).
(policy
  :schema-version 1
  :id "bifrost.security.ruby.network-xml-to-rexml-repeated-declaration-dos"
  :name "Network XML parsed by REXML 3.3.3 to 3.4.1"
  :message "A network response reaches REXML::Document.new on rexml 3.3.3 to 3.4.1, where repeated XML declarations make parsing do unbounded work (CVE-2025-58767); upgrade rexml to 3.4.2 or later"
  :severity warning
  :description "Reports a Net::HTTP.get response body that reaches the source argument of REXML::Document.new in a project whose Gemfile.lock selects rexml 3.3.3 to 3.4.1. In those releases a malformed document with repeated XML declarations is processed with unbounded work instead of being rejected, so whoever controls the document controls parse time (CVE-2025-58767, GHSA-c2f4-jgmc-q2r5). The version comes from Gemfile.lock; without a locked rexml version the run is incomplete rather than clean."
  :help-uri "https://github.com/ruby/rexml/security/advisories/GHSA-c2f4-jgmc-q2r5"
  :tags ["security" "ruby" "rexml" "denial-of-service" "cve-2025-58767" "cwe-400"]
  :analysis
    (analysis
      :type taint
      :mode may
      :call-modeling (call-modeling :unmodeled require-model)
      :sources
        (endpoint-set
          :entries [
            (source
              :id net-http-get
              :display-name "Net::HTTP.get response body"
              :categories [io.external network.response]
              :selector
                (rql
                  :schema-version 1
                  (language ruby
                    (resolved-call
                      :resolves-to member.web-network.ruby.net-http.get
                      :proof declared
                      (call-bindings
                        (call-shape
                          (call :callee (name "get")))))))
              :bind return-value
              :labels [attacker-controlled])])
      :sinks
        (endpoint-set
          :entries [
            (sink
              :id rexml-document-source
              :display-name "REXML::Document.new source"
              :categories [xml.parse resource.consumption]
              :selector
                (rql
                  :schema-version 1
                  (language ruby
                    (call-argument :formal-index 0
                      (resolved-call
                        :resolves-to member.ruby.rexml.cve-2025-58767.rexml-document.new
                        :proof declared
                        (call-bindings
                          (call-shape
                            (call :callee (name "new"))))))))
              :dangerous-operand (argument :index 0)
              :accepts [attacker-controlled])]))
  :classification
    (classification
      :fallback (classification-id :taxonomy "CWE" :id "400")))
